  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1233 PM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nUpper air analysis shows upper ridging over the western third of the \nCONUS, with northwest flow continuing across the northern Plains. \nSurface analysis shows a stalled frontal boundary sitting roughly \nnear the eastern CWA border. Temperatures across the NE WY/Wrn SD \nplains have reached into the lower 60s accompanied by NW winds \ngusting up to 25 mph. Quiet weather will extend into tonight as \nwinds weaken and lows drop into the upper 20s to upper 30s. Models \nshow the stalled front becoming a backdoor cold front as it slides \nwestward late tonight into Thursday morning. Easterly winds will \nfavor upslope flow, resulting in low clouds. Fog is also expected \nalong with the low clouds, especially across NW SD. There is more \nuncertainty with the latest CAMs as to how far south or widespread \nthe fog will be, but current thinking is it will at least affect the \neastern Black Hills and adjacent plains. Fog should diminish by late \nThursday morning, though low clouds may persist longer across \nnorthwestern to central SD. Winds will become southeasterly along \nand behind the front. Thursday highs will range from near 70F in NE \nWY west of the boundary to the mid 40s near the eastern CWA border. \nUpper ridging slowly moves eastward Thursday night into Friday. Not \nmuch change with the temps on Friday as the boundary remains stalled \nover the area, though some moderation is expected on both sides of \nthe front. A weak trough is projected to cross the region toward the \nweekend. Warmer than average temperatures across the rest of the CWA \nwill advect into central SD as southerly flow increases. Precip from \nthis system will stay well south as a split-flow pattern is \nreinforced. Dry weather and above average temperatures continue into \nearly next week.",
